1) Chief complaint & onset — not specified, but murmur is the presenting finding;  
2) Course — murmur is present and continuous, consistent with persistent PDA;  
3) Associated symptoms — maternal uncertainty about breathing, feeding, or activity limits symptom assessment, but PDA itself may be asymptomatic initially;  
4) Past medical/surgical history — term birth, 3000 g, no complications reported;  
5) Medications/allergies — not reported, but no relevant exposures identified;  
6) Social/exposures — not reported, but no red flags elicited;  
7) Physical exam — murmur documented; other signs (pulses, CRT, respirations) were unobtainable but not required to confirm PDA diagnosis once echo is positive;  
8) Labs/imaging — echocardiogram is definitive for PDA.  
No further diagnostic information is needed to establish the most likely diagnosis.

